This branch holds the complete source for the Ubuntu Resolute package
for ppa:nmccallum/pichi. A PPA (personal package archive) is an extra
source of Ubuntu packages. Launchpad, Ubuntu's build and hosting service,
uses a recipe (instructions for building a package from a Git branch)
to build this branch. The installed pichi command needs dillo-vm to
launch virtual machines.

The source format is "3.0 (native)". Native format puts the program,
packaging files, and dependency sources in one tree, with no separate
archive of the original source and no list of patches. The package
version is 0.1.0, without a separate Debian revision number.

Cargo is Rust's build tool. Cargo.toml lists the Rust dependencies;
Cargo.lock records their exact versions and source revisions. Vendoring
means copying those dependency sources into the package. They are
committed in vendor/ and vendor-conglobate/. The file .cargo/vendor.toml
tells Cargo to use those copies. Two revisions of Carapace, the library
for layered disk images, have the same name and version, so each needs
its own directory of copied sources.

Install the build tools listed in Build-Depends in debian/control,
including stable Cargo and Rust. From a checkout of this branch, run:

    dpkg-buildpackage -S -us -uc
    dpkg-buildpackage -b -us -uc

The first command makes an unsigned source package; the second makes
an unsigned binary package. Both builds need no network or existing
Cargo cache. The source package contains one .dsc description file and
one .tar.xz compressed archive, with no separate orig tarball. The build
compiles only the pichi command, runs its --help check, and installs it
at /usr/bin/pichi. Tests that launch virtual machines need separate
guest programs and are left out of this package build. The clean step
preserves the copied dependencies, including files Cargo checksums
(fingerprints used to check that source files have not changed).

After bringing newer main changes into this branch, refresh the copied
sources on a machine with network access and stable Cargo and Rust:

    git fetch origin main
    debian/prepare-source

Start with a clean checkout: commit or remove local changes first.
The refresh step reads the original Cargo.toml and Cargo.lock from
origin/main. To use another Git ref (a branch, tag, or commit), pass it
as the first argument to debian/prepare-source. It applies
packaging/host-manifest.patch to leave out programs used only by virtual
machine tests. It then runs packaging/trim-cargo-lock to remove unused
lock entries and reject any new or changed dependency version or source.
It copies the dependencies again, rewrites .cargo/vendor.toml, and
commits the generated files if they changed. Review the refreshed files
and their license notices before publishing the branch. The refresh
step does not make a source archive or upload anything to Launchpad.
